- in_text_citation: [(Gong et al., 2014-04)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/e337f440eeb76b53a684e5771c6b428352be249d)
- venue: Nature Communications
- citation count: 200
[method]
The paper describes a fluorescent voltage-imaging approach based on FRET-opsin sensors. Four sensor variants—Mac D139N (MacN), Mac D139Q (MacQ), Mac D139N-D150E (Mac-EEN), and Mac D139Q-D150E (Mac-EEQ)—were designed and screened for voltage sensitivity using 633-nm illumination and detection of near-infrared emission. The sensors were then characterized in cultured neurons, and optical voltage transients were detected from Purkinje neurons. This relates to the proposed method because both rely on genetically encoded fluorescent voltage indicators to optically measure neuronal membrane voltage, but the paper’s method is a sensor-development and characterization study rather than the proposed Voltron2-based 2-kHz imaging of CA1 pyramidal cells during head-fixed novel exploration.
[results]
The provided excerpt does not report quantitative outcomes such as voltage sensitivity values, signal-to-noise ratios, spike detection accuracy, or statistical comparisons. It only states that the voltage sensitivity of the four FRET-opsin variants was screened and that voltage transients were optically detected from Purkinje neurons.
[context]
Experimental conditions included 633-nm excitation and near-infrared emission detection, cultured-neuron characterization, and Purkinje-neuron voltage imaging. Animal experiments were approved by the Stanford University Administrative Panel on Laboratory Animal Care. The excerpt does not specify expression constructs, imaging frame rate, spatial resolution, behavioral paradigm, LFP recording, or CA1 pyramidal-cell targeting, so it does not directly establish the feasibility of the proposed Voltron2, 532-nm, 2-kHz CA1PC voltage-imaging experiment.
[end related work 1]

[start related work 2]
[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Hochbaum et al., 2014-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/a1370fe973b2e4762dadf90b74efef68a8d04f60)
- venue: Nature Methods
- citation count: 768
[method]
The paper used an Arch-based genetically encoded voltage indicator (QuasAr1/QuasAr2) and an all-optical patch-clamp construct (Optopatch) to measure membrane voltage. Arch mutant libraries were constructed and screened in E. coli, with random mutagenesis at Asp95 and Asp106, to generate QuasAr variants. QuasAr fluorescence was imaged in HEK293 and HeLa cells during voltage-clamp or induced transmembrane voltage, and in primary neuronal cultures and organotypic hippocampal slices during electrical stimulation or patch-clamp recording. Optopatch combined a QuasAr voltage sensor with an Arch/CheRiff actuator so that optical perturbation and voltage readout could be performed simultaneously. Action-potential timing was extracted from fluorescence traces and refined by subframe interpolation, while actuator photocurrents were fitted to quantify optical perturbation.
[results]
QuasAr2 showed improved voltage sensitivity relative to ArcLight A242, reported as approximately a threefold larger ΔF/F per 100 mV in cell-based comparisons. Optopatch recorded suprathreshold action potentials and subthreshold voltage transients in cultured neurons and organotypic slices, enabling spatially resolved measurements of action-potential propagation and synaptic transmission. Action-potential timing was resolved on the millisecond scale after subframe interpolation. The available paper excerpt does not provide exact ΔF/F values, timing precision, or statistical significance; the key reported outcome was feasible simultaneous optical perturbation and voltage measurement with improved sensitivity over ArcLight and improved AP timing estimation through subframe interpolation.
[context]
The experiments were in vitro: E. coli screening for Arch mutants, HEK293/HeLa cells for indicator characterization, primary neuronal cultures for parallel voltage imaging, and organotypic hippocampal slices for synaptic and propagation assays. The optical system used blue-light activation of Arch-based components, with a blue-shifted CheRiff actuator to separate perturbation from QuasAr readout, and fluorescence imaging was frame-rate limited, requiring subframe interpolation for AP timing. This relates to the proposed method because both use genetically encoded voltage indicators to capture sub- and suprathreshold membrane dynamics in hippocampal pyramidal-cell populations, but the paper's method is in vitro and uses QuasAr/Optopatch rather than Voltron2 in head-fixed mice exploring an air-lifted track.
[end related work 2]

