- in_text_citation: [(Piatkevich et al., 2019-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/18198ac751aa950b5b6a03d39432756282664e0b)
- venue: Nature
- citation count: 214
[method]
The paper used SomArchon-based in vivo optical voltage imaging to record single-cell, single-spike membrane potential dynamics with conventional wide-field microscopy. In hippocampal experiments, it combined intracellular membrane potential recordings with local field potential recordings and performed pair-wise coherence analysis between hippocampal neurons and LFPs. It also included head-fixed voluntary movement experiments, in vivo single-cell single-spike optical voltage imaging from striatal neurons during locomotion, and population imaging of spiking and subthreshold oscillations in hippocampal neurons. Virus delivery and expression were supported by AAV injection and in utero electroporation, with acute brain slice preparation used for some electrophysiological and fluorescence imaging experiments. This is directly relevant to the proposed research method because it demonstrates in vivo voltage imaging of sub- and suprathreshold activity, LFP-based oscillatory analysis, and head-fixed behavioral recording, all of which are central to the proposed Voltron2-based CA1PC voltage imaging during novel exploration.
[results]
The available section headings report that simultaneous recordings of intracellular membrane potential and LFPs revealed distinct phase relationships between spikes and theta oscillations in hippocampus. They also report that SomArchon enabled in vivo voltage imaging with single-cell, single-spike precision using conventional wide-field microscopy, and that population imaging captured spiking and subthreshold oscillations in hippocampal neurons. No quantitative values, effect sizes, sample sizes, statistical tests, jittered-control comparisons, or baseline comparisons are provided in the supplied excerpt.
[context]
The relevant experimental conditions included in vivo imaging in live mouse brain, acute brain slice electrophysiology and fluorescence imaging, AAV injection, in utero electroporation, head-fixed voluntary movement, and LFP recording. The imaging was described as conventional wide-field microscopy, but the excerpt does not specify laser wavelength, frame rate, spatial resolution, or whether the recordings were from CA1 pyramidal cells specifically. The paper’s relevant recordings were from striatal neurons during locomotion and hippocampal neurons, rather than explicitly from CA1 pyramidal cells during first-entry novel exploration. Therefore, the paper provides methodological precedent for voltage imaging, subthreshold/suprathreshold activity measurement, and LFP coherence analysis, but it does not directly validate the proposed Voltron2-based CA1PC place-field and ensemble synchrony protocol.
[end related work 5]

