- in_text_citation: [(Go et al., 2020-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/42ca87a196dfe6be7b80a87152a8394474a9f0de)
- venue: bioRxiv
- citation count: 30
[method]
The paper used a head-fixed, air-table floating track platform for two-photon calcium imaging of hippocampal CA1 in mice. Mice with hippocampal windows and GCaMP6s expression were water-restricted and trained twice daily for 45 minutes on either a floating circular track or a floating circular open field. The circular track had an outer diameter of 32.5 cm and a width of 5 cm; the open field had a diameter of 32.5 cm. The floating tracks were made of carbon fiber, weighed 100 ± 2.8 g, and had 4 cm high walls lined with phosphorescent visual cues and tactile cues. The air table rested under a two-photon resonant scanning microscope, and a magnet-based position tracking system recorded mouse coordinates and speed at 100 Hz. Water rewards were delivered through a lick spout attached to the headplate using a peristaltic pump. This method is directly related to the proposed air-lifted plastic track system because it demonstrates that head-fixed mice can be supported on a low-friction floating track while being imaged, enabling spatial exploration and place-field analysis. However, the paper used trained familiar tracks and calcium imaging rather than the proposed novel-room, corner-release, Voltron2 voltage imaging, and LFP recording.
[results]
Using this platform, mice initially showed minimal and irregular movement but adapted to smooth circular trajectories, reaching speeds up to 200 mm/s. Activity and average speed increased after about 9 training sessions, and by the end of training mice completed more than 100 laps in a 45-minute session. In the circular open field, the reward protocol produced meandering trajectories that sometimes stayed near the wall and sometimes crossed the arena. Locomotion speeds were similar to those in the circular track, but activity and average speed increased more slowly, requiring about 15 sessions. By the end of training, mouse trajectories covered more than 90% of the open field, divided into 2 × 2 cm bins, in a 45-minute session. The observed behavior resembled that of tethered and freely moving mice exploring circular arenas, supporting the conclusion that the surgical preparation and floating-track procedure did not adversely affect behavior.
[context]
Key experimental conditions included head-fixed mice with hippocampal windows and GCaMP6s expression in CA1, water restriction to 85% of pre-restriction weight, daily water intake limited to 1-3 mL, 45-minute training sessions twice daily, 11-14 circular-track sessions and 16-18 open-field sessions before imaging, infrared illumination and camera monitoring, 100 Hz position tracking, 4 µL water rewards, and imaging under a two-photon resonant scanning microscope. The track diameter was constrained by the distance from the microscope objective lens to the back wall of the microscope. For place-field analysis, only time points with animal speed above 20 mm/s were included, spatial bins were 2 cm on the circular track and 2 × 2 cm in the open field, and place cells were defined by spatial information above shuffled chance plus event-presence and occupancy thresholds. These conditions are relevant to the proposed method because they define the behavioral, hardware, and analysis constraints for head-fixed floating-track imaging, but they differ from the proposed plan in using calcium imaging, trained familiar environments, no separate-room habituation, no corner confinement, and no LFP or voltage imaging.
[end related work 2]

