[method]
The paper used a head-fixed floating circular track/open-field behavioral training and place-field analysis pipeline. Mice with hippocampal windows and GCaMP6s expression were water-restricted to 85% of pre-restriction weight and trained twice daily for 45 min on a 32.5 cm outer-diameter, 5 cm wide carbon-fiber circular track weighing 100 ± 2.8 g, or on a 32.5 cm circular open field, both mounted on an air table under a two-photon microscope. The environment was dark with infrared illumination, and the track walls were 4 cm high and lined with phosphorescent visual cues emitting at 500 and 520 nm plus tactile cues such as sandpaper, cardboard, foam, and bubble wrap; the open-field floor was lined with mesh tape. Mouse position and speed were tracked at 100 Hz using a magnet-based system. Water rewards of 4 µL were delivered through a lick spout and peristaltic pump: in the circular track, rewards were given at random locations for each completed loop; in the open field, rewards were given when the mouse crossed between the outer 6 cm annulus and two inner 20.5 cm half-circle regions, or vice versa. Calcium imaging movies were motion-corrected, ROIs were segmented, neuropil signal was removed, and fluorescence traces were deconvolved into event trains. For spatial analysis, only time points with running speed greater than 20 mm/s were included. Track position was linearized, 2-cm spatial bins and approximately 30 ms time bins were used, activity rate maps were boxcar-smoothed over three bins and normalized, and cells were classified as place cells if events occurred in at least half of laps, in at least 5% of moving time bins within the field, and spatial information exceeded the 99th percentile of 1,000 shuffled time stamps. Open-field maps used 2 × 2 cm bins, 2D Gaussian smoothing with a standard deviation of 1.5, and required events in at least 2% of moving time bins within the field. This method is related to the proposed novelty-control method because both use a head-fixed floating track and position/speed tracking, but the paper used repeated training in the same environment before imaging rather than first exposure in a separate room with initial corner confinement.
[results]
Mice initially showed minimal and irregular movement on the track but adapted to smooth circular trajectories, reaching speeds up to 200 mm/s and completing more than 100 laps in a 45-min session by the end of training. Activity and average speed increased after about 9 track sessions. In the open field, mice produced meandering trajectories resembling free and tethered exploration, with locomotion speeds similar to those on the track, and activity and average speed increased after about 15 sessions. By the end of training, trajectories covered more than 90% of the open field in 45 min. The place-field analysis framework identified spatially tuned CA1 cells using spatial information above shuffled chance levels, supporting the use of the floating track for spatial coding experiments, although the supplied text does not report the number of place cells or quantitative stability metrics.
[context]
The experiments were performed in head-fixed mice after viral injection and hippocampal window implantation, with imaging sessions limited to no more than 1 h per track. Mice were trained for 11-14 sessions in the circular track and 16-18 sessions in the open field before imaging; animals with poor GCaMP6s expression in CA1 were excluded. The air table was positioned under a two-photon resonant scanning microscope, and the track geometry was constrained by the distance from the objective lens to the microscope back wall. Data analysis used MATLAB, CaImAn, FISSA, OASIS, and custom scripts; neural activity rates were reported as F/F.s^-1 with 90% confidence intervals because they were approximately log-normal. The key condition relevant to the proposed method is that the paper did not keep the imaging environment novel: habituation and training occurred in the same floating track/open-field setup before imaging, whereas the proposed plan requires separate-room pre-training and first entry to the recording room with initial corner confinement to preserve novelty.
