  "contradictions": "The strongest contradiction, or more precisely the strongest evidence that the proposed method is not yet validated, is that [(Go et al., 2020-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/42ca87a196dfe6be7b80a87152a8394474a9f0de) used trained, familiar floating tracks and GCaMP6s calcium imaging rather than a genuinely novel recording room, corner release, Voltron2 voltage imaging, or LFP recording. Mice were water-restricted to 85% of pre-restriction weight, received 1-3 mL daily water, were trained twice daily for 45 minutes, and required 11-14 circular-track sessions or 16-18 open-field sessions before imaging; activity and average speed increased over training, with open-field coverage reaching more than 90% only after about 15 sessions. This contradicts the implicit assumption that a first exposure to the recording room after separate-room habituation will automatically produce stable, high-coverage exploration and mature place fields suitable for synchrony and spatial tuning analyses. The next strongest limitation is that the related work does not provide evidence for the proposed 2kHz Voltron2 voltage imaging in a head-fixed moving animal on an air-lifted track. [(Grosenick et al., 2017-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/283687587ba6e3834595946f74462c91e8085245) demonstrated approximately 100 Hz volumetric fluorescence imaging, not 2kHz voltage imaging, and did not report behavioral outcomes, ensemble sizes, jittered controls, or voltage dynamics; [(Go et al., 2020-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/42ca87a196dfe6be7b80a87152a8394474a9f0de) used two-photon calcium imaging, which is slower and not equivalent to sub- and suprathreshold membrane potential measurements. Therefore, the proposed combination of 532-nm excitation, 2kHz acquisition, high spatial resolution, and Voltron2 in a head-fixed track remains untested in these works. A weaker but important contradiction is that the position tracking in [(Go et al., 2020-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/42ca87a196dfe6be7b80a87152a8394474a9f0de) was 100 Hz, which may be insufficient for the proposed 10-30 ms population synchrony analyses and precise alignment of synchronous events with theta/ripple LFP and spatial tuning; the related work also did not record contralateral LFP, so it cannot support the proposed multi-modal correlation of synchrony with network oscillations. Finally, the related work used reward-based water delivery through a lick spout and peristaltic pump, with 4 µL rewards, whereas the proposed method does not specify reward, water restriction, or motivation; this is not a direct contradiction but leaves open whether the proposed novel exploration will generate comparable locomotion without the behavioral and motivational parameters that supported exploration in [(Go et al., 2020-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/42ca87a196dfe6be7b80a87152a8394474a9f0de).",
  "suggested_action": "The most important action is to adopt and adapt the validated hardware and behavioral constraints from [(Go et al., 2020-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/42ca87a196dfe6be7b80a87152a8394474a9f0de) for the proposed air-lifted plastic track: use a low-friction air-supported track with dimensions compatible with the microscope objective-to-back-wall distance, similar to the 32.5 cm outer diameter, 5 cm width, 100 ± 2.8 g mass, and 4 cm walls, and include visual/tactile cues to support orientation. The second most important action is to increase and synchronize position/speed tracking beyond the 100 Hz used in [(Go et al., 2020-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/42ca87a196dfe6be7b80a87152a8394474a9f0de), ideally to at least 200-500 Hz or matched to the 2kHz imaging frame rate, and to timestamp track position, LFP, and Voltron2 frames with sub-10 ms precision so that 10-30 ms synchronous ensembles, theta phases, and ripple events can be reliably aligned. The third most important action is to run a pilot validation of Voltron2 voltage imaging in the head-fixed track before the main experiment, measuring signal-to-noise, phototoxicity, motion artifacts, field of view, and spike/subthreshold detection at 532 nm and 2kHz, and to use acute slice or in vitro calibration if needed, because neither [(Go et al., 2020-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/42ca87a196dfe6be7b80a87152a8394474a9f0de) nor [(Grosenick et al., 2017-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/283687587ba6e3834595946f74462c91e8085245) demonstrates this specific voltage-imaging setup. The fourth action is to incorporate a behavioral baseline and, if scientifically acceptable, a short acclimation or repeated first-exposure design to account for the fact that [(Go et al., 2020-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/42ca87a196dfe6be7b80a87152a8394474a9f0de) required many sessions for stable exploration; if the goal is truly novel acquisition, collect multiple first exposures or early sessions and explicitly model the transition from immobility to locomotion rather than assuming trained-track behavior. The fifth action is to use the place-field analysis parameters from [(Go et al., 2020-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/42ca87a196dfe6be7b80a87152a8394474a9f0de) as a baseline, including excluding speeds below 20 mm/s, using 2 cm bins on a circular track or 2 × 2 cm bins in an open field, and defining place cells by spatial information above shuffled chance plus occupancy thresholds, while adapting these thresholds for voltage-imaged ensembles and jittered spike controls. The sixth action is to specify water restriction, reward, and motivation parameters, such as the 85% body-weight restriction, 1-3 mL daily water, and 4 µL lick-spout rewards used in [(Go et al., 2020-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/42ca87a196dfe6be7b80a87152a8394474a9f0de), or to justify why they are unnecessary for the proposed novel exploration. The least important but still useful action is to borrow computational extraction and validation practices from [(Grosenick et al., 2017-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/283687587ba6e3834595946f74462c91e8085245), such as deconvolution-based volume reconstruction, sparse structured matrix factorization, and acute slice validation, to improve cell identification and signal extraction from the high-speed Voltron2 data.",
